**Technical Specification Document: Maritime Operations for Oceanic Shipping Co.**

**Project Title:** Integration of Automated Container Handling Systems at Port of Long Beach

**Date:** October 15, 2023  
**Prepared by:** Maritime Operations Department, Oceanic Shipping Co.  
**Contact:** Jane Doe, Lead Project Manager (jdoe@oceanicshipping.com)  
**Reference Number:** OS-2023-LOGB

**1. Objective:**  
The purpose of this document is to outline the technical specifications and requirements for the implementation of an Automated Container Handling System (ACH) at the Port of Long Beach (POLB) to enhance operational efficiency and reduce turnaround times for vessels such as the MV Pacific Star and MV Atlantic Wave.

**2. Requirements:**

**2.1 System Capabilities:**  
- The ACH must support a minimum of 1,500 TEUs (Twenty-foot Equivalent Units) per hour to align with POLB's throughput targets for 2024.
- Integration with existing Terminal Operating Systems (TOS) such as Navis N4, utilized by Long Beach Container Terminal (LBCT) and TraPac, is mandatory.
- The system must be capable of real-time tracking of container movements, interfacing with Global Positioning Systems (GPS) and the Port Management System (PMS) overseen by the City of Long Beach.

**2.2 Equipment Specifications:**  
- Automated Guided Vehicles (AGVs) must be acquired from Konecranes, with a capacity to lift up to 40 tons.
- The solution requires at least four quay cranes, provided by ZPMC, with the ability to operate simultaneously to cater to the needs of vessels such as the MSC Oscar and Evergreen Ever Given.

**3. Constraints:**

**3.1 Environmental Regulations:**  
- Compliance with the California Air Resources Board (CARB) emissions regulations, including the use of zero-emission vehicles for all AGVs by 2025.
- Noise control measures must be implemented to adhere to local community standards.

**3.2 Operational Dependencies:**  
- The successful integration of the system depends on collaboration with the International Maritime Organization (IMO) for compliance with safety regulations.
- Coordination with shipping lines such as Maersk and Hapag-Lloyd is essential to ensure alignment of schedules and cargo handling protocols.

**4. Timeline:**  
- Project kickoff: November 1, 2023  
- Phase 1 (Site Preparation): November 2023 - January 2024  
- Phase 2 (System Installation): February 2024 - June 2024  
- Phase 3 (System Testing): July 2024 - August 2024  
- Full operational deployment: September 1, 2024

**5. Conclusion:**  
Successful execution of this project will position Oceanic Shipping Co. as a leader in maritime operations efficiency, leveraging advanced technologies to optimize container handling processes at the Port of Long Beach while ensuring compliance with all relevant regulations and stakeholder requirements.